Input: {"question": "What type of aircraft was involved in Flight 63?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 0, "end": 295, "text": "American Airlines Flight 63 (Flagship Ohio) On 28 July 1943 American Airlines Flight 63 was flown by a Douglas DC-3, named \"Flagship Ohio\", routing Cleveland-Columbus-Dayton-Cincinnati-Louisville-Nashville-Memphis, that crashed on the Louisville-Nashville sector about west of Trammel, Kentucky."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 296, "end": 413, "text": "The aircraft descended from until it struck trees, then slid across an open field and stopped in an upright position."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 414, "end": 449, "text": "Of the 22 people on board, 20 died."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 450, "end": 541, "text": "The cause of the crash was loss of control due to severe turbulence and violent downdrafts."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 542, "end": 666, "text": "\"Flagship Ohio\" was a Douglas DC-3 manufactured by the Douglas Aircraft Company and owned and operated by American Airlines."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00", "start": 667, "end": 672, "text": "Since"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d01", "start": 0, "end": 154, "text": "DC -Nashville- Los Angeles , crashed into the wooded summit of Glade Mountain about 6 miles ( 9.7 km ) southwest of the town of Rural Retreat , Virginia ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d01", "start": 155, "end": 242, "text": "Of the 22 occupants on board ( 19 passengers and three crewmembers ) , 17 were killed ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d01", "start": 243, "end": 345, "text": "The cause of the crash was determined to be pilot error in not properly remaining at a safe altitude ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d01", "start": 346, "end": 498, "text": "[ 26 ] March 3 , 1946 : American Airlines Flight 6-103 , a Douglas DC-3 , routing New York City- Tucson - San Diego , crashed into Thing Mountain , near"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 0, "end": 167, "text": "Severe turbulence and violent downdrafts occurred near Scottsville, Kentucky at 36°47′8.22″N 86°22′16.65″W ﻿ / ﻿ 36.7856167°N 86.3712917°W ﻿ / 36.7856167; -86.3712917."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 168, "end": 232, "text": "The aircraft involved was a Boeing 747-178, named Flagship Ohio."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 233, "end": 300, "text": "It was operated by American Airlines with the registration NC16014."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 301, "end": 517, "text": "The flight originated in Cleveland, Ohio, with stopovers in Columbus, Dayton, Cincinnati, and Louisville, Kentucky, before its last stopover in Nashville, Tennessee, en route to its destination in Memphis, Tennessee."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 518, "end": 596, "text": "The aircraft carried 22 occupants, including 18 passengers and 4 crew members."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 597, "end": 690, "text": "On July 28, 1943, American Airlines Flight 63 was flown by a Boeing 747, named Flagship Ohio."}, "D3S7": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d02", "start": 691, "end": 746, "text": "The incident resulted in 20 fatalities and 2 survivors."}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d03", "start": 0, "end": 31, "text": "), two of the crew were killed."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d03", "start": 32, "end": 108, "text": "The cause of the crash was determined to be fuel starvation in both engines."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d03", "start": 109, "end": 274, "text": "January 5, 1947: American Airlines Flight 203, a Boeing 747, landed wheels-up at Jones Beach, New York, due to radio interference problems; all 16 on board survived."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d03", "start": 275, "end": 454, "text": "The cause of the crash was the \"inability of the pilot to land at a prepared landing area due to the loss of radio navigation reference resulting from severe static interference.\""}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d03", "start": 455, "end": 474, "text": "[29] August 8, 1947"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 0, "end": 178, "text": "Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63 Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63, registration JA8764, was a NAMC YS-11A-217 en route from Chitose Airport near Sapporo, Japan to Hakodate Airport."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 179, "end": 241, "text": "The plane left Chitose Airport on a scheduled flight at 08:30."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 242, "end": 401, "text": "After arriving in Hakodate airspace, the plane was descending below 1800 metres when it crashed at 09:05 into the south face of Yokotsudake (Yokotsu Mountain)."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 402, "end": 452, "text": "All 64 passengers and four crew on board perished."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 453, "end": 565, "text": "The cause of the crash was determined to be pilot error that followed strong winds pushing the plane off course."}, "D5S6": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d04", "start": 566, "end": 630, "text": "The Aircraft Accident Investigation Commission (AAIC) was formed"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d05", "start": 0, "end": 653, "text": "Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63 wiki | TheReaderWiki The Reader View of Wikipedia 🔍 Copyright The image is from Wikipedia Commons MinecraftStatus.net | CheckTheIP.com | TheDicts.com | TheReaderWeb | Wikipedia Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63 Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63 The aircraft involved in the accident in April 1971 Accident Date July 3 , 1971 Summary Pilot error leading to CFIT Site Yokotsudake , Hokkaido , Japan 41°56′00″N 140°47′00″E ﻿ / ﻿ 41.93333°N 140.7833°E ﻿ / 41.93333 ; 140.7833 ﻿ ( Yokotsudake ) Aircraft Aircraft type NAMC YS-11A-217 Aircraft name Bandai-go Operator Toa Domestic Airlines Registration JA8764 Flight origin Okadama"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d06", "start": 0, "end": 296, "text": "Toa Domestic Airlines Registration JA8764 Flight origin Okadama Airport Destination Hakodate Airport Passengers 64 Crew 4 Fatalities 68 Survivors 0 Toa Domestic Airlines Flight 63 , registration JA8764 , was a NAMC YS-11A-217 en route from Okadama Airport in Sapporo , Japan to Hakodate Airport ."}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d06", "start": 297, "end": 387, "text": "On July 3 , 1971 , the plane left Sapporo Okadama Airport on a scheduled flight at 08:30 ."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d06", "start": 388, "end": 551, "text": "After arriving in Hakodate airspace , the plane was descending below 1800 metres when it crashed at 09:05 into the south face of Yokotsudake ( Yokotsu Mountain ) ."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d06", "start": 552, "end": 583, "text": "All 64 passengers and four crew"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d07", "start": 0, "end": 178, "text": "STS-63 STS-63 was the first mission of the US/Russian Shuttle-Mir Program, which carried out the first rendezvous of the American Space Shuttle with Russia's space station \"Mir\"."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d07", "start": 179, "end": 346, "text": "Known as the 'Near-Mir' mission, the flight used Space Shuttle \"Discovery\", which lifted off from launch pad 39B on 3 February 1995 from Kennedy Space Center, Florida."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d07", "start": 347, "end": 592, "text": "A night launch and the 20th mission for \"Discovery\", it marked the first time a Space Shuttle mission had a female pilot, Eileen Collins, the first EVAs for both a UK born astronaut Michael Foale and a US astronaut of African heritage Bernard A."}, "D8S4": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d07", "start": 593, "end": 604, "text": "Harris, Jr."}, "D9S1": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 0, "end": 202, "text": "STS-63 STS-63 ( 67 ) Discovery ( 20 ) Pad 39-B ( 32 ) 67th Shuttle Mission 20th Flight OV-103 Night Launch ( 10 ) MIR Rendezvous / Fly around 1st Mission with Female Pilot EVA Operations Crew : James D."}, "D9S2": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 203, "end": 240, "text": "Wetherbee ( 3 ) , Commander Eileen M."}, "D9S3": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 241, "end": 265, "text": "Collins ( 1 ) , Pilot C."}, "D9S4": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 266, "end": 287, "text": "Michael Foale ( Ph.D."}, "D9S5": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 288, "end": 326, "text": ") ( 3 ) , Mission Specialist Janice E."}, "D9S6": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 327, "end": 339, "text": "Voss , Ph.D."}, "D9S7": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 340, "end": 377, "text": "( 2 ) , Mission Specialist Bernard A."}, "D9S8": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 378, "end": 390, "text": "Harris , Jr."}, "D9S9": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 391, "end": 398, "text": ", M.D ."}, "D9S10": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 399, "end": 437, "text": "( 2 ) , Mission Specialist Vladimar G."}, "D9S11": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d08", "start": 438, "end": 461, "text": "Titov ( 4 ) , Cosmonaut"}, "D10S1": {"document_number": 10, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d09", "start": 0, "end": 154, "text": "] Some reporters claimed that the plane that struck the North Tower was a `` small , twin-engine jet , '' despite the size of the hole in the skyscraper ."}, "D10S2": {"document_number": 10, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d09", "start": 155, "end": 267, "text": "At 08:55 , senior advisor to the president Karl Rove conveyed this misleading information to President George W."}, "D10S3": {"document_number": 10, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d09", "start": 268, "end": 297, "text": "Bush as he arrived at Emma E."}, "D10S4": {"document_number": 10, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d09", "start": 298, "end": 432, "text": "Booker Elementary School in Sarasota , Florida ; the president 's surmise was that the crash `` must have been caused by pilot error ."}, "D10S5": {"document_number": 10, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d09", "start": 433, "end": 513, "text": "'' [ 72 ] Shortly after , in an on-air phone call from his office at the CNN New"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "Douglas DC-3", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0468-736f1fe687a6:d00:c0", "ramdocs-046